<Page
  title="The standard every instrument meets"
  description="What a case must publish, what is checked mechanically, and the judgements a checker deliberately refuses to make."
  current="/standards/"
>
  <h1>The standard<br /><em>every instrument meets</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This is the contract. An instrument that satisfies it can be published here; one that
    does not, cannot. It is stated so that someone else could build to it without anyone
    reading their code.
  </p>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Gates<span>the load-bearing rule</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Thresholds are fixed before results are seen</h2>
      <p>
        Every instrument declares, in advance, what would count as its model working and
        what would count as it failing. Those thresholds live in version control, and the
        commit that set them is dated before the run that tested them. <b>That is checkable
        by you</b> — the repository is at <a href="https://github.com/taufik-adinugraha/openstudy">github.com/taufik-adinugraha/openstudy</a>,
        and <code>git log --reverse -- cases/fire-haze/pipeline/validate.py</code> dates the
        thresholds against the gate record they produced,
        and it is the single reason anything else here is worth believing.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>A gate is published with its outcome</b> — pass, fail, or not evaluated —
          never as a count. "Three of four passed" hides which one failed.</li>
        <li><b>A failure ships red and stays.</b> It is not retuned, not quietly dropped,
          and not moved to a footnote.</li>
        <li><b>A gate whose input never arrived ships as not evaluated</b>, with the reason
          and what a person would have to do. It is never recorded as a pass.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          The instruments on this site currently include a nowcast that is <b>41% worse
          than doing nothing</b>, two hard gates that <b>fail</b> on a transport model, and a
          detector whose R² is <b>−11.09</b> before calibration. All three are on their own
          pages, stated in those words.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Baselines<span>the second rule</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Scored against a rival that already knows the easy part</h2>
      <p>
        A model is only interesting relative to what a person could do without it. So every
        instrument is scored against the naive rivals that already contain the obvious
        knowledge — carrying yesterday forward, the seasonal average, the published index
        that already exists — computed on training folds only, never on the test set.
      </p>
      <div class="aside">
        A fire model that predicts "September in Riau" and nothing else posts an excellent
        score and is worth nothing to anybody, because everyone already knows about
        September. The baseline is what separates the two cases, and choosing a weak one is
        the most common way to publish a result that is not there.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Publish<span>what a case must contain</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>The contract, in six items</h2>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td>
              <td><b>Gates with explicit outcomes.</b> Every gate carries an id, what it
                tested, its threshold, and its result.</td>
              <td class="yes">mechanically</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td>
              <td><b>A stated vintage.</b> When each source was retrieved, per source — not
                one date for the case.</td>
              <td class="yes">mechanically</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3</td>
              <td><b>Sources with licences.</b> Every input named, with its terms, where a
                reader can find them.</td>
              <td class="yes">mechanically</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4</td>
              <td><b>No personal data.</b> In any published file, not just the visible page.</td>
              <td class="yes">mechanically</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5</td>
              <td><b>No redistributed source data.</b> Derived results only; the pipeline
                fetches the rest.</td>
              <td class="yes">mechanically</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6</td>
              <td><b>An article separable from the dashboard.</b> One is frozen and
                citable, the other is live.</td>
              <td class="yes">mechanically</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Those six are run as a checker over every case. It is the acceptance test: produce
        output that passes, and the instrument can be published. This page used to add
        "no code review required, because what is checked is the output, not the
        implementation." <b>That was wrong, and this site disproved it.</b> A queue bug in
        the fire-haze pipeline scored one headline comparison on 133,386 cell-days while the
        figure beside it used 2,142,680 — and that output passed all six rules, because
        wrong answers are not malformed. What caught it was reading the job ledger row by
        row, which is code review. The six rules are a floor, not an acceptance test.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="sources">
    <div class="rail">Sources<span>public, not open</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <h2>Why this site says "public data" and not "open data"</h2>
      <p>
        It used to say open. It is not, in the strict sense. Everything is <b>publicly obtainable</b>, and much
        of it needs a free account and an API key. Those are different claims, and the
        difference matters to the one person who tries to re-run something.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What it takes to obtain</th><th>Which sources</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>A free account and a key</b></td>
              <td>NASA Earthdata and LAADS (night lights, fire detections), Copernicus
                (Sentinel-1, ERA5, CEMS, GLO-30), BPS (the official statistics every case
                is calibrated against), UN Comtrade</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Nothing at all</b></td>
              <td>GDELT's open feeds, Zenodo deposits, geoBoundaries, HDX, Google Open
                Buildings, OpenStreetMap</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="no"><b>A paid subscription, since 2026</b></td>
              <td>EOG's VIIRS night-lights product, which moved behind a subscriber wall in
                June 2026. The pipeline switched to NASA Black Marble; EOG is now a manual
                annual cross-check only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="no"><b>No stated licence</b></td>
              <td>TransJakarta's own GTFS feed, used with attribution and written
                confirmation requested — the named exception to the rule above. Jakarta's
                BPBD flood-history polygons carry no licence text either</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Excluded on licence</b></td>
              <td>Meta's Relative Wealth Index and the SMERU poverty map (CC BY-NC), GADM
                (non-commercial), and several GFW layers licensed "CC BY 4.0 excluding
                Indonesia" — which is exactly the jurisdiction this site works in</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>The exclusions cost something, and one of them cost a result.</b> Dropping
          Meta's wealth index and the SMERU map removed the poverty case's planned
          independent cross-check against a <em>different</em> construct. A temporal
          hold-out replaced it, which tests whether the same construct is stable over time —
          not the same thing. A reader was right to say that substitution was announced
          rather than argued.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="dim">
        The requirement for a contributor is therefore not "open data". It is
        <b>data you can lawfully share, from a source whose terms are stated</b> — which is
        what <a href="/contribute/#new">the path that asks for data</a> now says.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="unverifiable">
    <div class="rail">Unverifiable<span>what you cannot check yet</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <h2>The part of this page you have to take on trust</h2>
      <p>
        Everything above argues that the work should be believed because it can be checked.
        For a long time the objects required to check it were not published, which made the
        argument unfalsifiable. Three of the five things listed here have since been fixed;
        the list stays, with what changed, because a standard nobody can hold you to is not
        one.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>Fixed — the code is public.</b>
          <a href="https://github.com/taufik-adinugraha/openstudy">github.com/taufik-adinugraha/openstudy</a>, 226 commits, MIT.
          The commits that date the thresholds can now be inspected by anyone, the footer's
          licence points at something obtainable, and the gate records are in the tree
          rather than only behind a dashboard. A separate commercial product built by the
          same person is not in it, and is not part of this site.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><b>What is still true, and what a reader still cannot check:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>The gate records are in the repository but not deposited anywhere citable.</b>
          There is no DOI on them, so they can be read but not cited as a fixed object.</li>
        <li><b>The provenance manifests carry fetch timestamps but no content hashes</b>,
          no commit reference and no software versions. A timestamp establishes that a clock
          was read, not which bytes arrived.</li>
        <li><b>There is no pinned environment and no workflow engine.</b> A Makefile is not
          a lockfile: "re-runnable from raw source" is a claim about one machine until there
          is a container digest and a DAG behind it.</li>
        <li><b>No external reviewer has checked any of it.</b> The adversarial review on
          each case is done by the person who built it. That is careful self-editing, and it
          is not the same epistemic object. The repository does not fix this — it only makes
          it possible for someone else to.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>One anomaly, disclosed before anyone finds it.</b> In the rice case the
          published record carries a timestamp about two hours <em>earlier</em> than the
          commit that set its thresholds. That is almost certainly a re-run ordering
          artefact rather than tuning after the fact — but it is exactly what an audit of
          the pre-registration claim should catch, and pointing at it is cheaper than being
          caught by it. The repository lets you judge that yourself, which is the point.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>What is left, in order:</b> content hashes in every manifest; a pinned
          environment and a workflow engine; the gate record deposited with a DOI; and one
          external reader who is not the author. The first three are work. The fourth is an
          invitation, and now an actionable one — the code is there, so
          <a href="/contribute/#attack">breaking a gate</a> no longer requires asking
          anybody's permission.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="visualization">
    <div class="rail">Visualization<span>the criterion most often skipped</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>A static figure is not an instrument</h2>
      <p>
        This is a standard on the artifact, not a bar on the person: it says what the
        visualization has to do, and anyone whose work does it has cleared it. Most
        published analysis stops at a chart, which is why the last stage is written down
        as carefully as the gates.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Required</th><th>What it rules out</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Linked views.</b> Selecting in one view filters the others, so a
                reader can follow a single unit through every panel</td>
              <td>A grid of unrelated charts that happen to share a page</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Time is navigable</b> where the data has time — play it, scrub it, or
                step it, at the resolution the pipeline actually produces</td>
              <td>One year chosen by the author and presented as the picture</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Drill-down to the unit of analysis.</b> If the model estimates at
                kecamatan level, a reader can reach one kecamatan</td>
              <td>A national aggregate that cannot be questioned locally</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>At least one assumption is a control.</b> The reader changes it and
                watches the answer move — the threshold, the baseline, the window.
                <b>Where the scenarios are precomputed rather than refitted live, the page
                must say so</b>: indexing a set of author-chosen scenarios is weaker than
                recomputation and should not be described as if it were the same thing</td>
              <td>A conclusion the reader can only accept or reject whole</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Failures are visible in the interface</b>, not only in the appendix.
                A failed gate appears where the result appears</td>
              <td>A dashboard that looks equally confident whatever the gates said</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Legible without its author.</b> Units, dates, sources and the meaning
                of every encoding are on screen</td>
              <td>A figure that needs a caption the reader does not have</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>Why this is a standard and not a preference.</b> Every item above exists so a
          reader can disagree with the result in a specific way. A visualization that cannot
          be interrogated asks for trust instead of offering evidence — which undoes the
          gates and the baselines that come before it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="dim">
        Accessibility counts as part of it: keyboard reachable, contrast that holds, motion
        that respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, and a page that works on a phone.
      </p>

      <h3>These six are a floor, not a boast</h3>
      <p>
        Every criterion above is an affordance a charting library hands you — linked views
        are one selection plus a filter transform, a time scrubber is a slider bound to that
        filter, drill-down is a click handler on a choropleth. So the word "advanced" does
        not belong on a list of library features. What follows is what would actually be
        hard, stated so the standard can be held to something rather than flattered by it.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What would be genuinely hard</th><th>Where this site stands</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Live recomputation.</b> Refitting the estimator in the browser under a
                threshold the reader chose — a compiled model, or a server round-trip with a
                job queue</td>
              <td class="no">Not done. The scenarios are <b>precomputed</b>, so a reader
                indexes choices the author made in advance. That is weaker, and the pages
                now say so</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Out-of-core interactive rendering.</b> Millions of rows at sixty frames
                a second needs GPU aggregation or a tile pyramid</td>
              <td class="no">Not done, and deliberately opted out of — three instruments
                render on 2-D canvas with no WebGL, because the reader's own machine may
                have none</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Multi-resolution streaming</b> of a multi-gigabyte volume or trajectory:
                chunked storage, range requests, a level-of-detail pyramid</td>
              <td class="no">Not done. Existing tools do this far better</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Uncertainty encoded honestly</b> — ensemble spread, hypothetical outcome
                plots, reliability diagrams that do not flatter calibration</td>
              <td>Partly. Intervals are published and one case ships a reliability curve;
                the standard above does not require it, and should</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Projection and areal correctness</b> — equal-area for area statistics,
                incidence-angle normalisation for radar</td>
              <td>Done in the pipelines, absent from this standard</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Perceptual colour</b> — colour-vision-safe, luminance-ordered, never a
                rainbow ramp for a quantity</td>
              <td>Done in practice, absent from this standard</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="dim">
        Two of those six belong in the standard and are missing from it. Three are not
        claimed anywhere on this site. One is a real gap between what the criteria say and
        what the artifact does. Naming them is cheaper than being told.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Judgement<span>what a checker will not decide</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Five questions no regular expression should answer</h2>
      <p>
        These matter more than the six above, and none of them is mechanically decidable.
        They are recorded as <b>undecided</b> rather than guessed at, and answered by a
        person — or, for a first pass, by a model whose answer is labelled as such.
      </p>
      <ol class="jl">
        <li><b>Framing.</b> Is the finding stated as what <em>this run</em> found, rather
          than as a verdict on someone else's work?</li>
        <li><b>Baseline.</b> Does the rival it was scored against actually contain the easy
          knowledge?</li>
        <li><b>Failures.</b> Are failed gates presented as plainly as the passes, or
          buried?</li>
        <li><b>Named actors.</b> Does the case accuse a named company or project of
          wrongdoing?</li>
        <li><b>Claim support.</b> Does the headline follow from what the gates actually
          establish?</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="aside">
        The checker that enforces the six mechanical rules produced <b>three hundred false
        findings</b> on its first run, and needed four rounds of correction — a field named
        "name" is not personal data, a gate id in an HTML attribute is not visible to a
        reader, an outcome recorded as a boolean is still an outcome. That is precisely why
        it refuses to opine on the five above.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Honest limit<span>where this stops working</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>What none of this catches</h2>
      <p>
        A plausible question about a topic a dataset half-covers can pass every gate and
        still be answered wrongly. Rigour about method does not confer rigour about
        meaning, and no amount of validation makes a signal exist where there is none.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which is why the interpretation is not the measurement side's to write, and why an instrument
        published without one says so on its face rather than filling the gap with
        confident prose.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

